I think the OP is more interested to know _why_ Nethack is best played
without savescumming. (It's not important that the game itself tries to
prevent it. As most of us are likely to play this open source game on
our own computers we could very easily savescum anyway.)

This has to do with the nature of the game. In the case of single-player
RTS for example I would save after every mission, but would consider it
boring to save at the turning points of a particular mission--it just
removes too much elements (dynamics, surprise, etc.) from the game.

In nethack, the ascension is one big project/mission and every action
may change the situation. Many interesting concepts and the need for
strategy will be removed with savescumming: cursed items, insta-deaths,
identifying, random outcome of actions, combat-strategy ...

This pretty much holds for other RPGs, but here are differences, e.g.:

1) Some games require much more time to complete than Nethack, mostly
because of the (graphical) interface, the need of lengthy talks with
NPCs, etc. In this case, even if the game wasn't divided into
semi-independant quests it would make sense to save, esp. as the
replayability value is typically very low. You don't want to spend
another 6 hours talking to the same people, traversing the same paths, etc.

Nethack, OTOH, is typically finished in a few thousandscores of turns.
Should the character die, the new game will be just as interesting as
everything will be different because of random dungeon generation.

2) In many games there are visual and audial aesthetics as well as an
interesting plot to follow. People who can't/don't want to immerse
themselves in the gameplay can almost "skip" it by savescumming and
enjoy the rest.

Nethack has none of the above merits, it concentrates on pure gameplay
at which it is arguably one of the best. We may call this "gameplay
aesthethics". If you sacrifice them through savescumming there will be
not much left: the game will be boring.

3) Nethack is also a very tough game without savescumming. If one
savescums for the sole purpose of ascending and bragging about it,
people here won't like it because his achievement is actually a much
lesser one.

All in all, IMO everyone should chose for themselves how to play. The
important thing is to have fun. It just happens that

- with games which have independant parts and/or very low replayability
value saving makes things less boring.

- if the player thinks gameplay is boring/too hard, but is interested in
visuals, etc., he can eliminate much of it with savescumming. This might
be a bit schizo as the player won't be nearly as glad just to see/hear
the special effects without playing, but it's what many people seem to like.

- to play nethack with savescumming eliminates too much of the whole
game. Ascensions with savescumming are easy (yet boring) and that's why
people don't like reading them.

